<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1" name="viewport"/><meta content="#0d47a1" name="theme-color"/><meta content="Week 1 — Academic English Foundations: formal style, register, precision and diagnostic writing for MSc Electrical Engineering" name="description"/><title>Week 1 | Academic English Foundations</title><link href="/style.css" rel="stylesheet"/><style>.paper-phrase{font-weight:800;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-thickness:3px;text-decoration-color:#f2b705;text-underline-offset:4px;text-decoration-skip-ink:auto}</style></head><body class="week-page"><div class="wrap"><div class="site"><header id="top"><div class="ey">University of Kufa · Electrical Engineering Department · English for Master</div><h1 class="hero">Week 1</h1><p class="deck"><strong>Academic English Foundations: Formal Style, Register, Precision &amp; Diagnostic Writing</strong><br/>Academic English for MSc Electrical Engineering students.</p><div class="actions"><a class="btn" href="/">← Dashboard</a><a class="btn alt" href="/syllabus.html">Syllabus</a><a class="btn" href="/sources.html">English Resources</a><button class="btn gold" onclick="window.print()">Print / Save PDF</button></div></header><nav aria-label="Week 1 navigation"><a class="nav-brand" href="#top">Week 1</a><a href="#focus">Key Focus</a><a href="#outcomes">Outcomes</a><a href="#agenda">120-min Plan</a><a href="#academic">Academic English</a><a href="#register">Register</a><a href="#precision">Precision</a><a href="#real-paper">Real Paper</a><a href="#watchlist">Common Errors</a><a href="#practice">Practice Lab</a><a href="#diagnostic">Diagnostic</a><a href="#peer">Peer Review</a><a href="#reading">Extra Reading</a><a href="#exit">Exit</a></nav><main><section><div class="source"><strong>Basis for this lecture:</strong> the existing course material <em>Descriptive Language in Academic Writing for Engineers</em>. Its central language principles are <strong class="key">formal tone</strong>, <strong class="key">objectivity</strong>, <strong class="key">precision</strong>, <strong class="key">accurate technical vocabulary</strong>, <strong class="key">clear sentence structure</strong>, and appropriate grammatical choices. Week 1 introduces these foundations; later weeks develop them in depth.</div></section><section id="focus"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">Key focus</span><h2>Five Ideas to Remember</h2></div><p>These are the messages students should remember after the first lecture.</p></div><div class="focus-strip"><div class="focus-item"><b>1. Formal</b><span>Academic English should not sound <strong>casual</strong> or conversational.</span></div><div class="focus-item"><b>2. Objective</b><span>Use <strong>evidence</strong>, observations and measured results.</span></div><div class="focus-item"><b>3. Precise</b><span>Prefer <strong>numbers, units and exact terms</strong> over vague impressions.</span></div><div class="focus-item"><b>4. Clear</b><span>Good academic English is <strong>easy to follow</strong>, not unnecessarily complicated.</span></div><div class="focus-item"><b>5. Technical</b><span>Use the <strong>correct disciplinary term</strong>, not a rare synonym chosen only to sound advanced.</span></div></div><div class="lecture-callout" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>Key message for students:</strong><p><strong>Academic English does not mean difficult English.</strong> It means language that is <strong>appropriate, accurate, precise, clear and evidence-based</strong>.</p></div></section><section id="outcomes"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">Learning outcomes</span><h2>By the End of Week 1</h2></div><p>Students establish a common Academic English standard and identify their personal language priorities.</p></div><div class="outcomes"><div class="outcome"><b>Recognise</b><span><strong class="key">formal</strong> vs <strong class="key">informal</strong> academic register.</span></div><div class="outcome"><b>Revise</b><span><strong class="key">vague</strong> engineering statements for greater <strong class="key">precision</strong>.</span></div><div class="outcome"><b>Control</b><span><strong class="key">tone</strong> by reducing colloquial and emotive language.</span></div><div class="outcome"><b>Diagnose</b><span>personal priorities through a short <strong class="key">technical paragraph</strong>.</span></div></div></section><section id="agenda"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">Exact timing</span><h2>120-Minute Lecture Plan</h2></div><p>Teaching, guided practice, diagnostic writing, peer review, and the required 10-minute rest.</p></div><div class="agenda"><div class="slot"><span class="time">0–10</span><div><strong>Welcome + diagnostic discussion</strong><br/>What makes <strong>English at MSc level</strong> difficult?</div></div><div class="slot"><span class="time">10–18</span><div><strong>Course orientation</strong><br/>Syllabus, <strong>assessment</strong>, website and expectations.</div></div><div class="slot"><span class="time">18–33</span><div><strong>What is Academic English?</strong><br/><strong>Register, formality, objectivity, clarity and precision.</strong></div></div><div class="slot"><span class="time">33–48</span><div><strong>Formal vs informal language</strong><br/>Identify and improve <strong>electrical-engineering sentences</strong>.</div></div><div class="slot"><span class="time">48–60</span><div><strong>Quick diagnostic exercise</strong><br/>Select stronger <strong>academic alternatives</strong> and explain why.</div></div><div class="slot break"><span class="time">60–70</span><div><strong>REST — 10 min</strong><br/>No teaching.</div></div><div class="slot"><span class="time">70–83</span><div><strong>Precision + cautious claims</strong><br/>Use <strong>quantities, units, comparison, conditions and hedging</strong>.</div></div><div class="slot"><span class="time">83–98</span><div><strong>Diagnostic writing</strong><br/>Write a <strong>120–150 word</strong> engineering paragraph under baseline conditions.</div></div><div class="slot"><span class="time">98–108</span><div><strong>Peer review</strong><br/>Rate <strong>register, precision, objectivity and clarity</strong> as Yes / Partly / No.</div></div><div class="slot"><span class="time">108–118</span><div><strong>Rewrite + exit ticket</strong><br/>Improve two sentences and identify one <strong>specific Week 5 target</strong>.</div></div><div class="s